    commentary: "Matthew's carefully structured genealogy — three groups of fourteen generations — signals that Jesus is the climax of Israel's entire story, not an interruption of it (Wright). The four women named in the genealogy (Tamar, Rahab, Ruth, Bathsheba) hint at a grace that always worked beyond expected boundaries, preparing readers for a Messiah who would eat with sinners (Keener). Joseph's righteousness is shown not in rigid rule-keeping but in compassionate discernment — he sought to protect Mary even before he understood the full picture (Henry). The Magi's star-following worship contrasts sharply with Herod's murderous fear, illustrating that those far from Israel may respond more openly to the Messiah than insiders (Barclay). Matthew's repeated phrase 'to fulfill what the Lord had spoken' frames Jesus not as a surprise but as the destination toward which all of Israel's history had been travelling.",
