    commentary: "Jesus's baptism is not an act of personal repentance but of solidarity — he enters the waters with sinful Israel to identify fully with the humanity he came to save (Wright). The Father's declaration 'This is my Son, whom I love' at the baptism echoes Psalm 2 and Isaiah 42, crowning Jesus as both messianic King and suffering Servant (ESV Study Bible). The three temptations replay Israel's forty-year wilderness test, but where Israel failed, Jesus succeeds by trusting every word from the Father's mouth (Henry). Satan's offer of 'all the kingdoms of the world' reveals that the cross, not conquest, is God's chosen path to a different kind of kingdom (Barclay). The immediate response of Peter, Andrew, James, and John to 'follow me' shows that Jesus's call carries its own transforming authority — it is not merely an invitation but a summons.",
