    whatIsHappening: "Jesus closes the Sermon on the Mount with vivid warnings: judge carefully, ask persistently in prayer, walk the narrow road, test prophets by their fruit, and build your life on his words or watch it collapse. The crowds are astonished — he teaches with authority, unlike the scribes. Chapter 8 shows that authority in action: Jesus heals a leper with a touch, heals a centurion's servant from a distance at the officer's extraordinary faith, calms a storm with a word, and casts out demons who recognise exactly who he is.",
    keyVerse: { text: "Everyone who hears these words of mine and puts them into practice is like a wise man who built his house on the rock.", reference: "Matthew 7:24" },
    commentary: "The two builders parable ends the Sermon not with a call to agree with Jesus but to obey him — hearing without doing is structural foolishness, whatever our intellectual conviction (Henry). The centurion's faith is perhaps the most praised in any Gospel: a Gentile soldier grasps, better than most Israelites, that Jesus's word alone has authority to command healing across distance (Keener). When Jesus touches the leper he breaks multiple purity laws by contact — the point is that his holiness is so powerful it flows outward to cleanse rather than being contaminated by uncleanness (Wright). The storm-stilling provokes the disciples' question 'What kind of man is this?' — Matthew wants that question to linger in the reader's mind as the answer becomes clearer across the Gospel (Barclay). The demons' confession 'Son of God' is accurate but useless — knowledge of Christ that does not produce submission is spiritually dangerous.",
